WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.801
-Our next guest is a talented
actor and writer you know

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.870
from shows like "It's Always
Sunny in Philadelphia."

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.540
He stars in Season 4
of "A.P. Bio,"

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.942
which is now streaming
on Peacock.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:11.010
Let's take a look.
-That's the thing with cults.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.378
I mean, they do
start out idyllic.

00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:14.947
I mean, who doesn't love
dancing and fresh lettuce.

00:00:14.947 --> 00:00:16.749
Look, we need year five
of a cult,

00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:19.386
where there's a bunch of
pantless children running around

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:22.122
firing guns, and everyone's
foraging for Dumpster lettuce.

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:25.257
We've got to make Durbin think
you guys are way more disturbed.

00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:27.293
-Maybe we could hang
after school,

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:28.661
try to dig a little deeper?

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:30.797
I recently realized
I've been lying to myself

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:33.165
for years
about having shredded abs.

00:00:33.165 --> 00:00:34.600
I don't.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.036
-Please welcome back to the show
Glenn Howerton.

00:00:37.036 --> 00:00:39.672
How are you, Glenn?
-Good, buddy.

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:41.207
How are you?
-I'm great.

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:42.608
It's always delightful
to see you.

00:00:42.608 --> 00:00:45.778
I want to talk about the show,
but I really want to talk,

00:00:45.778 --> 00:00:48.681
before we get to it, about the
new additions to your family.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:52.318
-Ah, the doggies?
-Yeah, you have two dogs.

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:55.788
And this is new for you
in your life.

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:59.592
-Yes, this is a very new thing
for me.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.327
I grew up with cats.

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:03.729
I've never had a dog
in my entire life.

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:07.267
And my wife talked me into it.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.469
And she talked me into one,

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:11.204
and then she,
a couple months later,

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:12.672
talked me into
getting a second one.

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:15.141
Now she's talking about a third.
And I'm like, "Stop.

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.844
Stop it. It's too much."
Come on, you know.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:20.313
-How much of a gap between
the first talking-into

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:22.315
and the second talking-into
was there?

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:25.952
-I'd say, about
maybe eight months, tops.

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.553
We had the first dog
for about eight months

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:29.589
before I got talked into
a second dog.

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.056
-But, overall,
what would you say

00:01:31.056 --> 00:01:33.359
is the big difference, being a
cat person all your life,

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:35.494
and now entering
the canine world?

00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:38.464
-Truth be told, I've done
a complete and total 180.

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:40.866
I used to ---
I used to like cats,

00:01:40.866 --> 00:01:42.702
because, I don't know, I just
thought it was kinda cool

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:44.370
that cats were so standoffish

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.105
and you had to kind of
seduce a cat.

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:48.307
You know, cat's not just
gonna come to you.

00:01:48.307 --> 00:01:50.610
You have to kind of like
play games with the cat.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.578
I thought that was
kinda sexy and fun.

00:01:52.578 --> 00:01:55.849
But with dogs -- I mean,

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:57.217
I always thought, with dogs,

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:58.917
I'm like, "Too easy.
It's too easy."

00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:01.186
They run up to you,
they slobber all over you,

00:02:01.186 --> 00:02:02.722
lick you, jump on you.

00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:04.156
You know, like,
"Get your dirty paws off me.

00:02:04.156 --> 00:02:05.791
You're a little desperate."
You know what I mean?

00:02:05.791 --> 00:02:08.060
-Yeah, yeah.
You have two boys as well.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.328
We have that in common.

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:10.796
You mentioned
they're getting
older.

00:02:10.796 --> 00:02:16.001
My kids -- My oldest is 5 1/2.
So there's nothing he is into

00:02:16.001 --> 00:02:19.438
in regards to youth culture
that I don't understand yet.

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:22.341
But I feel like you're --
How scary is it

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:24.510
when you realize that you don't
know what they're talking about?

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.914
-[ Laughs ]
The most recent thing was,

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:32.551
my kids suddenly were like,
"Dad, that's so sus.

00:02:32.551 --> 00:02:33.819
That's so sus."

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:36.922
I was like, "What is
that word you're using?

00:02:36.922 --> 00:02:38.657
Sus? What are you saying?"

00:02:38.657 --> 00:02:41.660
And apparently,
"sus" means, like, "suspect."

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:42.962
-Oh, man.
-"It's very suspect."

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:44.463
That's what they were saying.

00:02:44.463 --> 00:02:46.966
So it's like --
It's every generation.

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:50.169
You get into your 40s,
and all of a sudden,

00:02:50.169 --> 00:02:52.805
you just -- you have
so many responsibilities,

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.140
you got so much going on,
I can't keep up

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.710
with what the hell all the kids
are talking about these days.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.145
-And you really, at that point
when they use "sus,"

00:03:00.145 --> 00:03:02.982
you have two awful choices.
The first is just owning up

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:04.349
that you don't know
what "sus" means,

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:06.319
and the other is trying
to fake it till you make it,

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:09.588
which makes you seem even older
than you actually are.

00:03:09.588 --> 00:03:11.123
I think kids
can see right through that.

00:03:11.123 --> 00:03:13.192
-[ Chuckling ] Yeah.

00:03:13.192 --> 00:03:16.729
I think you're totally right.
At first, I thought, "'Sus' --

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:19.331
Do I need to suss out
what that means?

00:03:19.331 --> 00:03:21.000
Or is that what it means?

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.568
They don't know that word."

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:25.037
"I don't know
what you're saying."

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:28.908
-You -- On "A.P. Bio,"
congratulations on Season 4.

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:31.377
Obviously,
you're around child actors.

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:34.380
Well, not child actors, right?
They're not "kid" kids.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.049
Let's be honest.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:38.717
-Yeah, I mean, they were --
Some of them were

00:03:38.717 --> 00:03:42.155
in their late teens and
early 20s when we started.

00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:44.723
And now some of them are, like,
in their 40s or something.

00:03:44.723 --> 00:03:46.926
[ Laughter ]
They're all -- I think --

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:49.095
I do think a couple of them
are in their 30s, though.

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:51.997
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:55.367
Like, Nick, who plays Marcus --

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:57.770
that kid has to shave
like three times a day.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.138
-Yeah.
-It's crazy.

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:01.841
-His son just auditioned for us.

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:05.244
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:07.247
-Yeah. It's great, though.

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:09.181
But it's the same thing
with those kids.

00:04:09.181 --> 00:04:11.918
You know, they, like, sit
around in their little circles.

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:13.519
You know what I mean.
I hang out with the adults

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.054
'cause I don't know
what those guys are talk--

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:18.023
They're in their 20s and 30s,
and most of them are single,

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:19.558
and I don't know what
they're talking about.

00:04:19.558 --> 00:04:23.062
I can't follow.
-You know, I miss

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:24.964
having my writers
in the building,

00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:27.032
because they're still --
they're working remotely.

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:28.667
And that's
the most jarring thing,

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:30.603
when they're around,
is that reminder.

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.505
'Cause you actually feel young
as long as you're

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:35.307
never in the same room
as people younger than you.

00:04:35.307 --> 00:04:39.278
That's the key
to staying young at heart.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:41.113
-Do you have this thing
where, like -- I mean,

00:04:41.113 --> 00:04:43.382
I still, in some ways,
I can't accept the fact that --

00:04:43.382 --> 00:04:45.084
Like, I still feel
like I'm in my 20s.

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:47.687
And it's not until
I'm around people

00:04:47.687 --> 00:04:50.856
who are actually in their 20s
that I go, "Oh, no, no, no.

00:04:50.856 --> 00:04:53.426
I'm very, very old now.
I guess I'm old."

00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:56.195
-It's amazing that every --

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:57.697
I feel like you and I
are very similar.

00:04:57.697 --> 00:05:00.533
We're like, "Yeah, but we're
gonna be young forever."

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:03.368
And then it's like,
"No, we're not interested."

00:05:03.368 --> 00:05:04.604
[ Laughs ]
-No.

00:05:04.604 --> 00:05:05.838
And you know,
I do a pretty good job

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:07.540
of keeping up with
what's going on with music,

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.942
or so I think.
I don't like a lot of pop music,

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:11.744
but I try to listen
to really cool stuff.

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:15.180
And I'll play it in the car,
but my kids make me turn it off

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.582
as we're pulling up
to the school,

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:18.217
'cause they don't want
the door opening

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:20.052
and whatever I'm blasting
out of my car to come out.

00:05:20.052 --> 00:05:21.487
I'm like, "This is
cool stuff, man.

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:22.988
This is, like, good music."

00:05:22.988 --> 00:05:24.590
-Yeah, I feel like,
even with the music,

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.192
they only show us
the stuff that they don't want.

00:05:27.192 --> 00:05:30.729
It's not the Glengarry leads
that we get with new music.

00:05:30.729 --> 00:05:31.964
It's like, "You'll like this."

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:33.799
And you're like, "Hey,
it's the new music."

00:05:33.799 --> 00:05:35.601
And then they're like, "No,
that was the bad music."

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:37.936
[ Laughter ]
-I really appreciate

00:05:37.936 --> 00:05:39.706
you making a
"Glengarry Glen Ross" reference.

00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:41.641
-Well, 'cause that's what kids
are into.

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:43.042
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:47.379
-Yeah, the 1990s
"Glengarry Glen Ross"?

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:50.683
-They like the play.
-Right.

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:52.785
-So, you --
Speaking of, so, you --

00:05:52.785 --> 00:05:55.388
We talked about this,
that you were, you know,

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:58.023
formally trained as an actor,
went to Juilliard,

00:05:58.023 --> 00:06:00.960
and did some
really serious stuff,

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.163
and now not just with "A.P. Bio"
but with this incredible

00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:06.632
record-setting run
on "Always Sunny."

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:10.502
Are you surprised that your
sort of early acting chops

00:06:10.502 --> 00:06:14.507
have led down this comedy path?
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:16.175
What happens?
You know what i mean?

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:18.277
I was supposed to be
taken seriously.

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.679
I was on the path to becoming

00:06:20.679 --> 00:06:22.548
a very serious and
well-respected actor.

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:24.917
-[ Chuckles ]
-And then I decided

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:27.119
to be a clown.
No, I --

00:06:27.119 --> 00:06:32.124
When I look back on it now,
I realize I was in a group

00:06:32.124 --> 00:06:34.160
of, like,
very, very serious actors.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.262
I was always seeking out
the one or two actors

00:06:37.262 --> 00:06:40.132
who I could just make jokes with
and mess around with,

00:06:40.132 --> 00:06:43.269
because everything we were doing
was so serious.

00:06:43.269 --> 00:06:46.005
We were doing classical theater.
I actually do love that stuff.

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:49.275
But there's got to be,
like, a release valve.

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:52.110
And I'm firmly of the mind
that, like,

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:55.848
even when you're doing a drama,
acting is supposed to be fun.

00:06:55.848 --> 00:06:57.850
I could be wrong about this.
You know what I mean?

00:06:57.850 --> 00:06:59.485
If you asked Sean Penn
this same question,

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:01.420
he'd be like,
"Acting is not fun."

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:05.391
He does scenes where he, like,
lights his foot on fire

00:07:05.391 --> 00:07:08.795
to make himself actually in pain
and stuff in a scene.

00:07:08.795 --> 00:07:11.029
Whereas I think
it's supposed to be fun.

00:07:11.029 --> 00:07:12.565
Like, even when
you're in a drama,

00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:14.366
I think even if the character
is not having fun,

00:07:14.366 --> 00:07:16.602
the actor should be enjoying it
on some level.

00:07:16.602 --> 00:07:18.638
Otherwise, why am I doing it?
I don't know.

00:07:18.638 --> 00:07:21.641
-I think we're very lucky
you ended up on this path.

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:23.943
Also, I think
we're all very excited

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:26.879
about the fact that you guys
are now starting up again.

00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:29.848
And I would imagine,
having been lucky enough

00:07:29.848 --> 00:07:31.583
to talk to other people
who work on the show,

00:07:31.583 --> 00:07:35.254
the hardest, at this point, is
just coming up with ideas.

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:38.391
Once you have the idea,
it must execute pretty easily

00:07:38.391 --> 00:07:40.125
because of how well you know
each other and the characters.

00:07:40.125 --> 00:07:42.928
But just coming up with
those ideas now must be so hard.

00:07:42.928 --> 00:07:45.297
-Yeah, it's brutal.
I'm not gonna lie.

00:07:45.297 --> 00:07:47.533
It's absolutely brutal.
The 15th season

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:50.335
in the writer's room was like --
I mean, it really was

00:07:50.335 --> 00:07:52.572
that old thing of,
"No, we've done that.

00:07:52.572 --> 00:07:54.006
No, we've done that.
No, we've done that."

00:07:54.006 --> 00:07:56.041
And you can't --
The characters can't

00:07:56.041 --> 00:07:58.377
fundamentally change
who they are.

00:07:58.377 --> 00:08:00.612
So they can't just become
a different person.

00:08:00.612 --> 00:08:02.782
So you're sort of --
You're hemmed in a little bit,

00:08:02.782 --> 00:08:04.850
in terms of, like,
what the characters' attitudes

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:06.986
and what their point of view is.

00:08:06.986 --> 00:08:08.888
But, that being said, I mean,

00:08:08.888 --> 00:08:11.256
I took two years off
from the writers' room

00:08:11.256 --> 00:08:13.258
in season 13 and 14.

00:08:13.258 --> 00:08:16.428
And so I felt a little fresher
coming into it this year.

00:08:16.428 --> 00:08:20.733
And I think I can honestly say
this season of "Sunny"

00:08:20.733 --> 00:08:23.002
is probably gonna be our best
in many years.

00:08:23.002 --> 00:08:24.970
-That's really, really exciting.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:27.973
Congratulations on "A.P. Bio."
I can't wait for that.

00:08:27.973 --> 00:08:29.942
And it's always so great
to see you, buddy.

00:08:29.942 --> 00:08:31.410
-Thanks man.
Good to see you, too.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:34.179
Next time, hopefully, in person.
-I really hope that is true.

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:36.048
-"A.P. Bio,"
streaming on Peacock.

